6.0 ROBOTICS
Robotics is explicitly included as a major topic in the revised Zimbabwe Forms 5–6 Physics syllabus. The syllabus lists Principles of Robotic Systems, Robot Design Methodologies, and Robot Construction and Programming.

6.1 WHAT IS A ROBOT?
A robot is a programmable physical system capable of sensing its environment, processing information and producing actions through actuators.

A simplified robotic system contains:
sensors → controller → actuators → mechanical system,
with feedback from the environment.

Robots differ from simple automatic devices because they can be programmable, responsive and capable of combining sensing, decision-making and action.

6.2 PRINCIPLES OF ROBOTIC SYSTEMS
A robotic system should be analysed as:
input → processing → output.

Inputs are measurements from sensors.
Processing is performed by a controller or computing system.
Outputs are actions performed by actuators.

Feedback allows the system to compare actual conditions with desired conditions and modify its action.

6.3 SENSORS
Common sensors include:
• light-dependent resistors for light intensity;
• photodiodes for light detection;
• ultrasonic sensors for distance;
• infrared sensors for proximity;
• thermistors for temperature;
• switches for contact;
• encoders for rotational position;
• accelerometers for acceleration.

A sensor has a measurable response to a physical variable.

Important design issues:
range;
sensitivity;
resolution;
response time;
repeatability;
noise;
calibration.

6.4 ACTUATORS
Actuators convert electrical/control signals into physical action.

Examples:
DC motors;
servo motors;
stepper motors;
solenoids;
relays;
buzzers;
LED indicators.

Motor selection depends on torque, speed, power, control accuracy, supply voltage and mechanical load.

6.5 CONTROL
Open-loop control does not use feedback. The controller assumes that the commanded action produces the desired result.

Closed-loop control uses feedback to compare measured output with a target.

Example:
A robot maintaining a set distance from a wall can measure distance with an ultrasonic sensor and adjust motor speed based on the error.

6.6 DESIGN METHODOLOGY
A sound robot-design process can follow:
1. identify a real problem;
2. investigate the problem;
3. establish requirements;
4. generate alternative solutions;
5. select a suitable solution;
6. design the mechanical/electrical/software system;
7. build a prototype;
8. test;
9. evaluate against requirements;
10. refine;
11. document and present;
12. consider maintenance, safety, environmental and social effects.

This matches the heritage-based, project-oriented character of the revised syllabus.

6.7 ROBOT REQUIREMENTS
A specification should identify:
• task;
• operating environment;
• payload;
• speed;
• accuracy;
• endurance;
• dimensions;
• power source;
• safety;
• cost;
• materials;
• user interaction.

A good design is not simply the most complex design. It should meet requirements reliably with appropriate resources.

6.8 MECHANICAL DESIGN
Mechanical considerations include:
mass distribution;
centre of gravity;
wheel diameter;
traction;
gear ratio;
bearing friction;
frame rigidity;
load capacity.

For a wheeled robot, torque must be sufficient to overcome rolling resistance, friction and acceleration requirements.

6.9 ELECTRICAL DESIGN
Electrical design includes:
power supply;
motor drivers;
microcontroller;
sensors;
protection;
switches;
wiring;
ground/reference;
current requirements.

Motors may require substantially more current than a controller output can safely provide, so a motor driver or switching stage is normally required.

7.0 PROGRAMMING CONCEPTS
Robotic programs commonly use:
variables;
constants;
input;
output;
conditional statements;
loops;
functions;
timing;
state machines.

A simple autonomous control algorithm:
1. read sensor;
2. compare reading with threshold/target;
3. select action;
4. drive actuator;
5. repeat.

7.1 PSEUDOCODE
Pseudocode is a language-independent way of describing an algorithm.

Example:
START
read distance
IF distance < safe_distance
    stop
    turn
ELSE
    move forward
END IF
REPEAT

The algorithm should avoid ambiguous instructions.

7.2 SENSOR CALIBRATION
Calibration compares sensor readings with known or reference conditions.

A calibration graph can relate sensor output to physical quantity.

For a linear response:
output = gradient × input + intercept.

Calibration errors can produce systematic errors in robot behaviour.

7.3 MOTOR CONTROL
A motor controller may vary speed using pulse-width modulation (PWM).

A PWM signal switches rapidly between high and low states. The duty cycle controls the fraction of time the signal is high.

Average motor power can be controlled by duty cycle, although the exact motor response depends on the motor, driver and load.

7.4 ROBOTIC FEEDBACK
If target distance is d_target and measured distance is d_measured:
error = d_target − d_measured.

A controller can adjust motor commands according to this error.

More advanced control may use proportional, integral and derivative terms, but students should only apply the sophistication required by the prescribed course.

7.5 SAFETY
Robot design must consider:
moving parts;
sharp components;
battery hazards;
electrical short circuits;
heat;
unexpected motion;
software failure;
emergency stopping.

Testing should begin at low speed and low-risk conditions.

7.6 ENVIRONMENT AND HERITAGE
The revised syllabus is heritage-based and encourages connections between science, technology, community needs and national resources.

Suitable student projects could address:
• agricultural monitoring;
• water management;
• solar-powered systems;
• environmental monitoring;
• assistive devices;
• educational automation;
• waste sorting.

A project should be evaluated for usefulness, sustainability, affordability and safety.

7.7 DOCUMENTATION
A good robotics project record contains:
problem statement;
background research;
requirements;
design sketches;
circuit diagram;
mechanical design;
algorithm/flowchart;
program;
testing results;
failures;
modifications;
final performance;
evaluation;
recommendations.

7.8 TESTING
Testing should use measurable criteria.

Example:
Requirement: robot detects an obstacle within 30 cm.
Test at several known distances.
Record detection success/failure.
Repeat trials.
Calculate success rate.
Identify conditions causing errors.

7.9 EXAM AND PROJECT SKILLS
Students should be able to explain why a sensor is suitable, select an actuator, justify a control method, draw a system block diagram, interpret test data and suggest improvements.

A sophisticated robot is not automatically a good project. The design must solve the stated problem and demonstrate reliable evidence.

CORE CONCEPTS
sensor;
transducer;
controller;
actuator;
feedback;
open-loop control;
closed-loop control;
algorithm;
microcontroller;
PWM;
calibration;
prototype;
testing;
evaluation;
safety;
sustainability.

6.10 ROBOT DESIGN EXAMPLE
Problem: build a robot that detects obstacles and stops.
Input: ultrasonic distance sensor.
Controller: microcontroller.
Output: motor driver.
Actuators: two DC motors.
Algorithm:
read distance;
if distance is below threshold, stop;
otherwise move forward.
A stronger design also adds a turning/recovery routine rather than remaining permanently stopped.

6.11 FEEDBACK EXAMPLE
Target distance = 20 cm.
Measured distance = 25 cm.
Error = −5 cm if error is target minus measured.
The controller must decide whether to move closer based on its chosen sign convention.

6.12 PWM
A 1 kHz PWM signal with 40% duty cycle is high for 40% of each period. PWM can regulate motor speed through a driver. The motor's actual mechanical speed depends on load, motor characteristics and controller behaviour.

6.13 CALIBRATION EXAMPLE
If a sensor gives 0.5 V at 10 cm and 2.5 V at 50 cm under a linear approximation, the calibration slope is:
(2.5−0.5)/(50−10) = 0.05 V/cm.
The intercept and validity range must also be checked experimentally.

6.14 PROJECT EVALUATION
A project should compare measured performance with the original specification. If the specification required 90% obstacle-detection success and testing gives 72%, the correct conclusion is that the prototype needs improvement; the student should identify likely causes and propose specific changes.

6.15 POWER BUDGET
A robot designer should estimate current drawn by controller, sensors, motors and indicators. Battery capacity, motor startup current and driver losses must be considered. A supply that can provide the controller's nominal current may still fail when motors start.

6.16 MECHANICAL STABILITY
A low centre of gravity generally improves stability. Wheel placement affects turning and traction. Excessive payload can increase motor torque requirements and battery consumption.

6.17 SAFETY TESTING
First test software without mechanical load where possible.
Then test motors at low speed.
Use an accessible power switch.
Keep moving mechanisms clear of hands.
Secure the battery.
Test failure conditions deliberately and safely.

6.18 DESIGN TRADE-OFFS
Higher speed can reduce control time.
Higher torque may reduce speed.
More sensors can improve information but increase cost, wiring and software complexity.
A robust design balances these trade-offs against the specification.

6.19 FLOWCHART CONCEPT
Start → initialise → read sensor → decision → actuator command → repeat.
Every decision should have clearly defined yes/no branches.

6.20 ROBOTICS AND ZIMBABWEAN CONTEXT
A useful school project may be designed around irrigation monitoring, crop protection, water-level detection, solar-panel tracking, environmental measurement or assistive technology. The project should be adapted to local conditions, available materials, cost and maintainability rather than copying an imported design without evaluation.

ZIMBABWE FORMS 5–6 PHYSICS 2024–2030 — STUDY ROADMAP

This study pack follows the revised Upper Secondary Physics syllabus structure for Forms 5–6. The official syllabus is heritage-based and emphasises practical work, problem solving, ICT, project-based learning, environmental responsibility and connections between science, technology and Zimbabwean contexts.

THE EIGHT MAJOR TOPICS

1.0 GENERAL PHYSICS
Form 5:
1.1 Physical Quantities and Units
1.2 Errors and Uncertainties

2.0 NEWTONIAN MECHANICS
Form 5:
2.1 Kinematics
2.2 Dynamics
2.3 Forces
2.4 Work, Energy and Power
2.5 Circular Motion
2.6 Gravitational Field

3.0 OSCILLATIONS AND WAVES
Form 5:
3.1 Oscillations
3.2 Waves
3.3 Superposition

4.0 ELECTRICITY AND MAGNETISM
Form 5:
4.1 Electricity
4.2 D.C. Circuits
4.3 Electric Fields
4.4 Capacitance
Form 6:
4.5 Electromagnetism
4.6 Electromagnetic Induction
4.7 Alternating Currents

5.0 ELECTRONICS
Form 6:
5.1 Analogue Electronics
5.2 Digital Electronics
Communication concepts are integrated with electronics/modern-physics treatment where required.

6.0 ROBOTICS
Form 5–6 project-oriented treatment:
6.1 Principles of Robotic Systems
6.2 Robot Design Methodologies
6.3 Robot Construction and Programming

7.0 MATTER
Form 6:
7.1 Phases of Matter
7.2 Deformation of Solids
7.3 Temperature
7.4 Thermal Properties of Materials
7.5 Ideal Gases
7.6 Non-viscous Fluid Flow
7.7 Transfer of Thermal Energy

8.0 MODERN PHYSICS
Form 6:
8.1 Charged Particles
8.2 Quantum Physics
8.3 Atomic Structure
8.4 Radioactivity
8.5 Communication

HOW TO STUDY THE PACK
Begin with General Physics because units, vectors, graphs and uncertainties are used everywhere.
Then master Newtonian Mechanics because it establishes force, motion, energy and field reasoning.
Oscillations and Waves develops periodic motion, wave behaviour and superposition.
Electricity and Magnetism develops field and circuit models and then electromagnetic induction.
Electronics applies electrical principles to signal processing and control.
Robotics combines physics, electronics, programming, mechanical design and feedback.
Matter develops thermal, mechanical and fluid models.
Modern Physics introduces quantum, atomic, nuclear and communication concepts.

CROSS-CUTTING SKILLS
Every major topic should be revised through:
• definitions;
• equations;
• derivations;
• units;
• graphs;
• practical experiments;
• uncertainty;
• applications;
• assumptions;
• limitations;
• multi-step calculations;
• evaluation;
• safety;
• environmental implications.

MATHEMATICAL FOUNDATION
Students should be comfortable with:
standard form;
indices;
algebraic rearrangement;
ratios;
percentages;
trigonometry;
vectors;
simultaneous equations;
gradients;
areas under graphs;
exponentials;
logarithms;
inverse relationships;
quadratic relationships;
basic differentiation/integration ideas where they arise from physical relationships.

PRACTICAL FOUNDATION
Students should be able to:
read analogue and digital instruments;
choose a suitable measuring range;
record repeated measurements;
calculate means;
estimate uncertainties;
plot graphs;
draw lines of best fit;
calculate gradients;
interpret intercepts;
identify anomalous results;
evaluate systematic/random errors;
suggest specific improvements;
observe safety procedures.

EXAMINATION FOUNDATION
The revised assessment framework includes multiple-choice, structured, free-response, practical and school-based project components. Consequently, revision must cover recall, understanding, application, analysis, problem solving and practical reasoning.

IMPORTANT DATA
Use the constants supplied on the examination paper where they are provided. Common syllabus data include:
c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹
ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² F m⁻¹
e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C
h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s
R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹
N_A = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹
k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹
G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻²
g = 9.81 m s⁻².

FINAL PRINCIPLE
Physics is not a collection of disconnected formulas. Every equation describes a physical relationship under stated assumptions. A strong ZIMSEC answer identifies the physical model, selects the appropriate relationship, uses correct units and signs, shows sufficient working, and evaluates whether the result makes physical sense.
